1.ELEMENTS IN A RANGE 
Given an array arr[] containing distinct positive integers, and two integers start and end defining a range. Determine if the array contains all elements within inclusive range [start, end].
Note: If the array contains all elements in the given range return true otherwise return false.
SOURCE CODE : 
        class Solution {
            public boolean checkElements(int start, int end, int[] arr) {
                // code here
                HashSet<Integer> set=new HashSet<>();
                for(int num:arr){
                    set.add(num);
                }
                for(int i=start;i<=end;i++){
                    if(!set.contains(i)){
                        return false;
                    }
                }
                return true;
            }
        }
PROBLEM LINK : https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/elements-in-the-range2834/1


2.FIND UNIQUE ELEMENT 
Given an array of elements occurring in multiples of k, except one element which doesn't occur in multiple of k. Return the unique element.
SOURCE CODE : 
        class Solution {
            public int findUnique(int k, int[] arr) {
                // code here
                HashMap<Integer,Integer> map=new HashMap<>();
                for(int num:arr){
                    map.put(num,map.getOrDefault(num,0)+1);
                }
                for(int num:map.keySet()){
                    if(map.get(num)==1){
                        return num;
                    }
                }
                return -1;
            }
        }
PROBLEM LINK : https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/find-unique-element2632/1


3.PRINT LINKED LIST 
You are given the head of a singly linked list. Return an array containing the values of the nodes.
        /*
        class Node {
            int data;
            Node next;
            Node(int x) {
                data = x;
                next = null;
            }
        }*/

        class Solution {
            public ArrayList<Integer> printList(Node head) {
                // code here
                ArrayList<Integer> list=new ArrayList<>();
                Node temp=head;
                while(temp.next!=null){
                    list.add(temp.data);
                    temp=temp.next;
                }
                list.add(temp.data);
                return list;
            }
        }
PROGRAM LINK : https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/problems/print-linked-list-elements/1


4.SORT A STRING 
Given a string consisting of lowercase letters, arrange all its letters in ascending order. 
SOURCE CODE : 
        class Solution {
            public String sortString(String s) {
                // code here
                char c[]=s.toCharArray();
                Arrays.sort(c);
                String sortString=new String(c);
                return sortString;
            }
        }
